From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:35:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 160/163] fix(refinery): park the lane on a push veto instead
 of draining with assigned work (gcp-ubv2)

When the target branch is red, the rig's pre-push gate refuses every merge's
push and merge-push returns status 7. The patrol drained with the bead still
assigned, so the next fresh session re-selected the same head, was refused
again and drained again. On 2026-10-01 that walked feryn's refinery through six
restarts in an hour into the session circuit breaker and cost the lane 80
minutes, with the fix bead stuck behind it.

Status 7 now parks the lane (MAYOR RULING 4, option 1 scoped to status 7):

- merge-push.sh prints `merge-ff-push: REFUSED target=<T> sha=<S>` just before
  returning 7, naming the tip the target still stood at. merge-batch inherits it.
- New lane-wedge.sh. `mark` records the refused head in a marker in the clone's
  git dir, mails the mayor once per (target, sha) with the release (`rm <marker>`)
  and puts a note on the bead. The bead is kept: assignee and status unchanged,
  because releasing it would make a polecat redo a good branch. `check` holds
  while the target stands at the marked sha and clears the marker once it moves.
- The patrol's invoke block gains a 7) arm that runs `mark` and continues to
  patrol-summary, and STOPs and drains only if the marker was not written.
- find-work runs `check` and, while it holds, scans only beads labelled base-red
  and skips every head already refused, so a fix for the red target can land and
  nothing batches. An empty WORK takes the existing IDLE path.
- Registered lane-wedge.sh in the inference gate like merge-batch.sh.

Out of scope: run-tests reds, the pre-push gate reading known-red, and breaker
config.

Validation: test_refinery_lane_wedge.sh (new, 12 tests), merge_lanes (status 7
case flipped from drain to park), find_work (+5 wedge cases), batch_wiring (+1
case, 10 total) and the inference-gate pytest all pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
